// Figure 4.1 — Use case diagram
// Eraser has no native UML use-case type: actors are drawn as person icons, use cases as ovals,
// and the system boundary as a group. Eraser: New diagram > Flow chart, paste this code.
direction right

Analyst [shape: oval, icon: user, color: blue]
Administrator [shape: oval, icon: user-check, color: purple]
Transaction source (simulator) [shape: rectangle, icon: send, color: gray]
Inference service [shape: rectangle, icon: cpu, color: gray]

Payment Fraud Detection System [color: lightblue] {
  Sign in / sign out [shape: oval]
  Monitor live dashboard [shape: oval]
  List / filter / sort alerts [shape: oval]
  View alert and explanation [shape: oval]
  Record decision with note [shape: oval]
  Generate period report [shape: oval]
  View model health [shape: oval]
  Change a reviewed decision [shape: oval]
  Configure alert threshold [shape: oval]
  Manage users and roles [shape: oval]
  View audit log [shape: oval]
  Export analyst feedback [shape: oval]
  Submit transaction(s) [shape: oval]
  Score transaction [shape: oval]
  Raise alert [shape: oval]
}

// Analyst
Analyst - Sign in / sign out
Analyst - Monitor live dashboard
Analyst - List / filter / sort alerts
Analyst - View alert and explanation
Analyst - Record decision with note
Analyst - Generate period report
Analyst - View model health

// Administrator: everything the analyst can do (generalisation), plus administration
Administrator > Analyst: inherits
Administrator - Change a reviewed decision
Administrator - Configure alert threshold
Administrator - Manage users and roles
Administrator - View audit log
Administrator - Export analyst feedback

// System actors
Transaction source (simulator) - Submit transaction(s)
Submit transaction(s) --> Score transaction: «include»
Score transaction - Inference service
Raise alert --> Score transaction: «extend» probability ≥ threshold
View alert and explanation --> Inference service: explanation on first view
